lz6 lib/ v1.7.0 (tag v1.7.0, commit f0a101d, 2026-10-24: lz6's first stable release, formats
frozen since v1.6.11); before it v1.6.15-pre (b72a8fc) and v1.6.14-pre (3106c73): lz6.c, lz6hc.c,
lz6.h, lz6hc.h, lz6common.h, mem.h, copied unmodified. Its changes since v1.6.13-pre
are in the table of lz6's seq engine (levels 9 to 13 of the newer format); the HC table
of the LZ5 v1.5 "portable profile" that -ma:lz6 writes is untouched, and -ma:lz6 comes
out byte for byte the same at levels 0, 1, 5 and 9 to 15. 1.6.15-pre changes rows 3 and 10
of the same seq table (lz6's levels -1 and -8): -ma:lz6 at levels 0 to 15 writes the same bytes.
1.7.0 fixes two things -ma:lz6 meets: the HC parsers' 3-byte match test compared a pointer
built as start - 2^window, which wrapped with low buffer addresses (32-bit, no PIE or ASLR),
so the same input could give another (valid) stream in about 1% of the runs; and
LZ6_decompress_safe read one byte past a block of 1 byte or an empty input. At normal
addresses -ma:lz6 writes the very bytes of before (levels 0 to 15).

Before (never released: on main between 1.5.1 and 1.6.0): v1.6.13-pre (tag v1.6.13-pre, commit a082a99, 2026-10-22): lz6.c, lz6hc.c,
lz6.h, lz6hc.h, lz6common.h, mem.h, copied unmodified. Its changes since v1.6.12-pre are
in the encoder of levels 2 to 7 of lz6's seq engine (the newer format); the LZ5 v1.5
"portable profile" that -ma:lz6 writes comes out byte for byte the same at levels 0 to
15 (checked on two corpora, and on 64 MB blocks at levels 0, 9 and 15).

Before (zpaq-std 1.2.0 to 1.5.1): v1.6.12-pre (tag v1.6.12-pre, commit ecbbaa7,
2026-10-19), whose changes since 52076b2 were also in the seq engine; the same bytes.

Before (up to zpaq-std 1.1.0):
lz6 lib/, frozen at commit 52076b2 (lz6 master, 2026-09-27), with two HC fixes
found through zpaq-std: c483e96 "never write past the output buffer" (the HC
parsers could write more than LZ6_compressBound on data LZ cannot compress: 732 KB
past a 16 MB buffer) and 52076b2 "calloc the match-finder tables" (the parsers read
uninitialised table entries, so the output depended on the heap). Same output as a
fresh process with 1dd4bec whenever it fits (checked: 41 inputs x 9 levels, with
zpaq-std's limit). The other commits since 1dd4bec touch lz6's seq engine only.
Previously frozen at 1dd4bec (2026-09-25, the HC binary-tree fix: before it, HC
levels 13-15 were O(n^2) on a long repetitive run that does not reach the end of the
buffer, which every zpaq block is), and before that at a8e1b51 (v1.6.4-pre). Same output bytes for what zpaq-std uses:
checked against a8e1b51 on 36 inputs x levels 0-15 at window 22, 64 and 32 bits.
